-- 3
I think that the article presents an overly negative view of the effect of AI on higher education, but that might not be a bad thing, since most of the world seems to present an
overly positive view. I certainly agree that AI companies don't have the best interests of unversities or students in mind, and I suspect that many students of my generation
(hopefully not me!) will discover that they know much less than they think they did upon trying to apply the skills they faked through school in the workplace. That being said,
I think the article ignores the enormoous potential for learning acceleration that these tools offer. If all the models were capable of doing was rewriting a given text in a way
that preserved accuracy decently and was understandable to a particular user, there wouldn't be any question that they were an aide to the educational process. The problem is
that in addition to their helpful skills, they also come with a whole bunch of "trap" skills that, when used by a student, produce the sensation of heightened learning without
the actual effect or allow the student to gain unwarranted recognition. This is to say, if students as a group can learn to use the useful parts and not the not-useful parts,
then they'll come out ahead. I'd like to think that this is only a matter of time, but there may also be a world in which people just stop being intrested in learning, though
that's too sad to consider so I won't.
